On Sat, 2 Jul 2005, Karl von Laudermann wrote:

> 1) Assuming that it's a bad idea to identify scrolls and potions by just
> trying them out and seeing what happens, how do you get them identified?
> Is there another way besides waiting until a shop appears and then
> price-IDing a scroll of Identify? I suppose I could just read the
> spoilers and price-ID everything, but I'd rather find a way to actually
> *identify* my items, before using them.

If you want to be completely spoiled, read the object identification
spoiler (http://www.steelypips.org/nethack/id_faq.html). To be less
spoily, a few tips :

price-iding is very usefull, at least fo scroll of ID, but will not
precisely identify the object since many object cost the same price. Yet,
it help in many ways :
_ if you have already identified, many object of a given cost and find a
new one, you'll be able to have a good idea of what a new item is.
_ Prevent you from using the really dangerous items (scroll of amnesia) or
waste the powerfull ones (uncursed potions of gain ability).
_ Allow you to choose better the object you'll id with a scroll because
you now which one will be likely to be the object you need (ring of slow
digest or free action).

When price-iding is not available, here are some general comments :
try all non-cursed rings (and amulets). Wear them for 2 or 3 turns and
you'll sometime be able to id or pseudo-id them (some self-id, other have
obvious effect (ESP, regeneration). DANGER : rings of conflict and
powerfull peaceful (shopkeeper). So don't do it when close to a
shopkeeper... Or not with a ring likely to be conflict (once
price-ided...)

Rings can be (pseudo)-ided with sinks (but you loose the ring, so do it
only with duplicate or cursed rings) and wands can be (pseudo-)ided by
engraving rather than zapping (avoid the bouncing rays...) but this use 1
charge (mainly a problem with wand of death, WoW still provide a wish this
way).

Don't read cursed spellbook. Also avoid (costly) uncursed once when your
INT is low.

Reading scrolls is dangerous for mainly 3 reasons: amnesia, fire and
destroy armor. Before amnesia has been ided, don't read unknown scroll
likely to be it (that is, price-id before...) Before fire has been ided,
drop all flamable object (scrolls, spellbooks, potions, armor) before
reading unknown scrolls (likely to be fire). Before destroy armor has been
ided, only wear a piece of useles armor when reading.

Of course, this may be problematic if reading (cursed) create monster and
becoming surrounded by nasties will naked or if reading enchant armor and
having own piece of crap boosted... But since these scrolls do not belong
to the same price class, price-iding will reduce these risks.


Concerning potions, dipping items in them can help. First something
useless (in case it's polymorph). Then a throwable weapon (in case it's
sickness) and lastly a unihorn (cancel all other nasty potions). Unihorn
will also 'cancel' sickness but you may want to pseudo-id it more
reliabily (of course, price-id can also do this since it's the only nasty
in its price category).

The rest of the potions can be drunk almost safely (paralysis is still
very bad). Booze can also be canceled with some amethyst, but then you
need to identify amethist and gems should not be your priority when
identifying stuff. Invisibility and levitation can be bad in some cases,
so don't drink-id if you're not ready to face the consequences of these
ones.

Of course, the nasty potions cancelled by unihorn all have an effect which
is itself cancelled by unihorn, so you may want to drink them to really
id them and them apply your unihorn. But that's a waste of water (which
can be hard to find in the early game).

gems can be partly ided by engraving with them or by throwing them at
unicorns (preferabilty co-aligned but this is not necessary).

Don't forget that the game has a *great* note system : #name.

When price-iding, #name the items "FOOBIE - 254$" or something similar, so
you'll be able to get the information quickly if you're later searching
for a precise thing to id with a scroll.
Before dipping, #name the potions something like "orange - polymorph ?" if
the object is polyed, the potion will be pseudo-ided, if not, rename the
potion accordingly to your next test (or " " if you want to remove
completely the name).
Of course, you can combine that (eg "orange - 266$ - polymorph ?"), names
may be as long as you want.


Personally, I do have a sheet of paper where all objects are written, by
type and price value, with a non-erasable pen. Then, when I start iding
lots of items, I cross the name of the items I know with an erasalbe pen
(so I can easily reuse the sheet for the next game). This allow for an
easier price-pseudo-iding when few items remains in a given category. And
also allow me to choose more carrefully which item to identify with a
scroll, since I know in which category can be the one I really want. And
this prevent me from constantly looking price list in spoilers.


And for my more general technic of identification, I usually wait until I
can price id stuff before risking to read/drink unknown things. This mean
that I often do the mines first for the guaranteed general shop (or reach
the mall in slash'em).


Notice also that some objects (very few) have a special method of
identification (typically potions of oil or ring of mood on slash'em).

